    "text": "Period: 1990–Present\nHistory: Contemporary conducting embraces unprecedented diversity of background, repertoire, and style. Women and conductors of color have broken barriers, though full equity remains distant. The historically informed approach has influenced mainstream practice, while new music demands ever-expanding technical skills. Digital technology enables global collaboration and education. The conductor's role has evolved toward collaborative leadership, balancing artistic vision with orchestra democracy. Audiences expect dynamic programming, community engagement, and multimedia integration alongside musical excellence.\nKey developments: Growing (if incomplete) diversity in the profession, Digital streaming changes consumption patterns, COVID-19 pandemic disrupts and transforms concerts, Historical and modern approaches synthesize, New music requires extended techniques, Collaborative leadership models emerge, Social media and personal branding become essential\nTechnique: Integrate historical awareness with modern sensibility, Master diverse styles from Baroque to avant-garde, Lead with collaborative, transparent communication, Engage audiences through programming and presentation, Navigate digital platforms and virtual performance, Champion diversity in repertoire and personnel\nNotable conductors: Simon Rattle — b. 1955 — Berlin Philharmonic; LSO; educational initiatives; diverse programming\nMarin Alsop — b. 1956 — First woman to lead major American orchestra (Baltimore); mentor\nGustavo Dudamel — b. 1981 — LA Philharmonic; El Sistema; youth advocacy; Paris Opera\nMirga Gražinytė-Tyla — b. 1986 — City of Birmingham Symphony; rising star; diverse repertoire\nYannick Nézet-Séguin — b. 1975 — Philadelphia Orchestra; Metropolitan Opera; warmth and precision\nEsa-Pekka Salonen — b. 1958 — San Francisco Symphony; composer-conductor; contemporary music\nKlaus Mäkelä — b. 1996 — Youngest major orchestra director; Oslo Philharmonic; rising generation",
