    "title": "Early 20th Century",
    "text": "Period: 1900–1950\nHistory: The early 20th century saw conducting reach new heights of technical precision and interpretive depth. Toscanini's fierce fidelity to the score contrasted with Furtwängler's romantic flexibility, establishing a creative tension that defines conducting philosophy to this day. The recording era began, preserving conducting styles for posterity and enabling mass audiences. American orchestras rose to prominence, while European conductors fled fascism, enriching musical life worldwide. Conducting pedagogy formalized through institutions and master-apprentice relationships.\nKey developments: Recording technology preserves conducting interpretations, Debate: textual fidelity vs. romantic interpretation, American orchestras achieve world-class status, Refugee conductors transform Western musical culture, Radio broadcasts bring orchestral music to millions, Conducting pedagogy becomes more systematic, Women begin (slowly) to enter the profession\nTechnique: Develop crystalline clarity and precision, Balance objectivity with personal expression, Master the growing complexity of modern scores, Conduct for the microphone as well as the hall, Build orchestras through long-term relationships, Study scores with unprecedented rigor\nNotable conductors: Arturo Toscanini — 1867–1957 — Legendary precision and fidelity to the score; NBC Symphony\nWilhelm Furtwängler — 1886–1954 — Romantic interpretive freedom; Berlin Philharmonic\nBruno Walter — 1876–1962 — Warmth and humanity; Mahler protégé\nLeopold Stokowski — 1882–1977 — Orchestral showmanship; Philadelphia sound; Fantasia\nSerge Koussevitzky — 1874–1951 — Boston Symphony; championed American composers; Tanglewood\nOtto Klemperer — 1885–1973 — Monumental interpretations of German repertoire",
