    "pageTitle": "Conductors Reference Guide",
    "title": "Classical Period",
    "text": "Period: 1750–1820\nHistory: The Classical era saw conducting evolve from shared leadership to a more centralized role. Composers increasingly directed their own works, with Haydn leading from the keyboard and Mozart conducting opera from the fortepiano. The symphony orchestra standardized, requiring unified interpretation. By the early 19th century, the modern conductor began to emerge — standing before the orchestra with a baton, providing visual leadership for the entire ensemble. This transition reflected the growing complexity of orchestral music and the need for interpretive authority.\nKey developments: Transition from keyboard leadership to standing conductor, Baton use becomes more common, Composer-conductors establish interpretive authority, Orchestra size and standardization increase, Public concerts demand polished, unified performances, First dedicated conducting positions at major institutions, Silent, visual conducting replaces audible time-beating\nTechnique: Develop clear, economical beat patterns, Balance attention between sections, Shape phrases with graduated dynamics, Maintain steady tempo with tasteful flexibility, Coordinate between pit and stage in opera, Use facial expression and left hand for nuance\nNotable conductors: Joseph Haydn — 1732–1809 — Led the Esterházy orchestra and London symphonies from the keyboard\nWolfgang Amadeus Mozart — 1756–1791 — Conducted operas and subscription concerts throughout Europe\nLudwig van Beethoven — 1770–1827 — Conducted his own works until deafness prevented; expanded orchestral forces\nLouis Spohr — 1784–1859 — Credited with introducing the modern baton to London in 1820",
